MISSION: FAST.

# ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
#  Example 10 — Why the LLVM backend matters
#
#  This program does real work (50 million loop iterations with
#  arithmetic) — enough to make the performance difference between
#  the interpreter and a compiled binary obvious.
#
#  Try all three and compare:
#
#    time chloroplast run     examples/10_performance.plnt
#    time chloroplast compile examples/10_performance.plnt --run
#    time chloroplast compile examples/10_performance.plnt --backend c --run
#
#  On a typical machine:
#    interpreted        ~90 seconds
#    compiled (LLVM)    a few milliseconds
#    compiled (C)       a few milliseconds
#
#  Both compiled backends get this fast because LLVM's optimizer
#  (`opt -O2`, run automatically before `llc`) and gcc's optimizer
#  both recognize this specific accumulation pattern and fold it into
#  a closed-form calculation — this is a standard compiler optimization
#  (strength reduction / loop induction variable analysis), not a
#  special case written for this example.
#
#  For code with real control flow, function calls, and branching —
#  not just tight arithmetic loops — expect roughly 50-200x speedups
#  from compilation, since most of the interpreter's overhead comes
#  from re-parsing and re-dispatching each statement on every
#  execution, which a compiled binary does zero times at runtime.
# ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

1\ SHOW "Computing sum of 1..50,000,000...".

1\ CREATE sum(NUM) TO 0.
1\ CYCLE i FROM 1 TO 50000000,
2\   INCREASE sum BY i.
1\.

1\ SHOW "Sum: ".
1\ SHOW sum.
1\ SHOW "Done.".
